FUN_004431e8: zero-init the stack fd (uninitialized-read divergence)

The car-grid init builds a 0x2c-byte fd in local_74[0xb] (contiguous since FIX SFS)
but the per-level setters only write the strip hint (+0x22) and lane (+0x2a);
Search_For_Strip/FUN_00428678/Track_Follow then consume further fields (height slot,
the dword read at +0x27) that still hold stack garbage. gcc's native and wasm stacks
hold different garbage -> the init Track_Follow settled the cars/camera on different
strips (recorded_strips_ started +2 entries apart; car_fd byte +0x29 read 6 on wasm,
0 on native; instrumented builds flipped the value, proving the uninit read). The
dd2h reference capture (L2 frame 182) shows the consumed fields as 0 for every car
(car_fd+0x29 == 0, 0x77cf39 == 0), so explicit zeros reproduce the observable
original state and make the init deterministic across targets.

--- a/dd2.c
+++ b/dd2.c
@@ -31520,6 +31520,12 @@
     }
     goto LAB_004435ec;
   }
+  { int _k; for(_k=0;_k<0xb;_k++) local_74[_k]=0; }  /* zero the stack fd: Search_For_Strip/
+     FUN_00428678/Track_Follow consume fields the per-level setters never write (e.g. the height
+     slot and the byte under the +0x27 dword read); the ORIGINAL leaves them as its own stack
+     garbage, which the reference capture shows as 0 (car_fd+0x29 == 0 for every car, 0x77cf39 == 0).
+     Explicit zeros reproduce the observable original state and remove the uninitialized reads that
+     made native and wasm place the demo grid on different strips (recorded_strips_ +2 shift). */
   puVar8 = &SCA_Data;
   local_38 = 0;
   local_3c = 0;
